VulkanLumen — evidence-based Lumen scene-lighting and final-gather rewrite

This module follows the public Lumen architecture described in:
  Epic SIGGRAPH 2022 Lumen presentation (Daniel Wright et al.)
  https://advances.realtimerendering.com/s2022/SIGGRAPH2022-Advances-Lumen-Wright%20et%20al.pdf
  Epic Lumen Technical Details and Performance Guide (UE 5.8)
  https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/lumen-performance-guide-for-unreal-engine

The shared VulkanLumen public API and vulkan-experiment's frame loop are
unchanged. No new system/module was introduced outside lumen/.

IMPLEMENTED for the self-contained Cornell demonstration
  1. Primary visibility: existing raster HDR color, material face IDs, depth.
  2. Scene ray queries: 32 triangles / 63 BVH nodes, now INCLUDING the
     emissive ceiling panel. Shadow rays shorten their endpoint so the lamp
     does not self-occlude. BVH built on CPU and traversed on GPU.
  3. Surface material capture: 16 planar UV cards in a 256x256 RGBA8 atlas.
     For the Cornell room each surface is planar with uniform albedo; material
     normals and emission are read from the exact analytic face data.
  4. Surface lighting: TWO separate RGBA16F 256x256 card atlases:
       - direct lighting (emission + 2x2 visibility-tested area-light samples)
       - total cached radiance (emission/direct + previous world-cache bounce)
     One 64x64 card is refreshed per frame to amortize ray tracing.
     The primary raster uses bilinear DIRECT lighting from the card atlas;
     secondary rays sample TOTAL lit card radiance. No per-pixel shadow rays.
  5. World radiance probes: 12x8x12 first-order directional coefficients,
     updated with a configurable bounded budget and GPU any/closest-hit rays.
     Probe hits now fetch surface emission or total cached surface radiance,
     enabling amortized multibounce feedback across consecutive frames.
  6. Screen radiance probes: one probe per 16x16 internal pixels. Each owns
     an 8x8 octahedral DIRECTIONAL radiance atlas; update a default four
     directions per probe per frame, accumulating to 24 observations per bin.
     Screen-depth trace first, scene BVH fallback second, world-cache distant
     radiance on miss. Probe history is reused only for matching face/position.
  7. Final gather: integrate the 64 directional radiance bins with a cosine
     Lambertian kernel and octahedral solid-angle weighting. Compare it with
     the WORLD probe's hemispherical irradiance estimate (both E/pi units),
     then write their difference times surface albedo to screen_gather.
     Full-resolution composition interpolates only same-face, same-depth
     correction. When validation fails, original world-cache GI remains.
     The old single-ray arbitrary .12-clamped correction has been REMOVED.
  8. GPU timestamps: cards (material capture + direct/total surface lighting),
     world GI, primary raster, screen gather, composite and combined total.

FRAME PASS ORDER
  material cards (only when dirty)
  update one directly/indirectly lit surface card
  update bounded world-space radiance probes from lit card cache
  raster scene: cached direct lighting + world-space diffuse GI
  directional screen ray gather -> integrated same-unit irradiance correction
  final composition (tonemap)

PERFORMANCE AND INITIAL CONVERGENCE
  - Internal render size capped at 1280 pixels width on a Retina swapchain.
  - One 64x64 card refresh each frame. All 16 cards require at least 16 frames
    after startup or invalidation to populate. More frames accumulate radiance
    history and multibounce information.
  - Default screen rays = 4 of 64 directional bins per probe per frame;
    available in settings.screen_rays_per_probe, clamped to 1..8.
  - World updates remain 64 probes x 4 rays by default.
  - No full-screen pixel denoiser, neural reconstruction, or TAA.
  - These are first measurements to be made on M1; the previous 60 FPS
    Stage 7 timing is NOT evidence that this refactor also reaches 60 FPS.
  - The direct cache improves shadow softness by using four actual
    visibility-tested emitter samples PER CACHED SURFACE TEXEL, not one
    hard cutoff toward the light center per pixel.

IMPORTANT INCOMPLETE FEATURES (do not confuse with working Epic Lumen)
  - The Cornell demo uses analytic flat cards. Automatic mesh-card placement,
    coverage, streaming and capture for arbitrary mesh materials are not done.
  - set_scene()/set_lighting()/set_gbuffer() reject nonempty external inputs
    rather than silently pretending to use them. Their binary contracts and
    game-scene integration must be implemented in a later scoped milestone.
  - BVH remains a CPU-built static Cornell tree; no GPU BLAS/TLAS update,
    global SDF hierarchy or dynamic mesh transforms.
  - World probes still use a fixed room grid, first-order SH, and simple
    normal-biased interpolation: no reliable per-probe depth visibility,
    relocation or camera-centered clipmaps. Light leaking can still occur.
  - Screen probes have a limited depth march and 4-direction/frame sampling;
    no adaptive placement or screen-probe reprojection into camera motion.
  - No separate dynamic-light update system, complete surface-space radiosity
    scheduling, physically based multilobe materials or specular reflections.
  - results().diffuse/reflections are not ready as reusable standalone images;
    the Cornell demo composites its indirect lighting internally.
  - Not equivalent to Epic's complete Lumen renderer or its quality modes.

TEST (from vulkan-experiment/)
  git pull --ff-only
  rm -f c.lock
  c build run vulkan-experiment

Expected shader output: lumen_cards.comp, lumen_surface_lighting.comp,
lumen_radiance.comp, lumen_screen.comp, lumen_geometry.{vert,frag},
lumen_composite.{vert,frag}. The startup banner should report
"Lit Surface Cache + directional Screen Radiance Cache" and 32 triangles.
Allow 5-10 seconds stationary convergence. Report screenshot and GPU
cards/GI/scene/screen/composite/total timings; compare against Stage 7.

All changes in this refactor remain inside vulkan-systems/lumen/. The next
request for a new reusable system or general-scene backend should be scoped
and reviewed separately before implementation.
